window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-9b546d8180a26105"]={"key":"ny_main-9b546d8180a26105","group":"ny_main","uid":"9b546d8180a26105","rank":100,"title":"kestrel terminals monitoring transition decision memo","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are Nadia Marchetti, a first-line supervisor at Kestrel Terminals, and Cecilia Gauthier in Legal and Katarzyna Voskresensky in contracting need a briefing-ready decision memorandum for approval this week. Kestrel Terminals is midway through replacing fixed guard posts with Bramblewood Advisory’s remote-monitoring model. The January–March and April–June incident records appear to show a material change, but the monitoring transition also changed what gets detected, classified, and logged. During the transition, a rare armed intrusion occurred while the monitoring system was unavailable, exposing a serious risk that the transition draft does not address. Ivo Cordova and Kaia Yamamoto are waiting for an operating position that can be translated into an executable plan. There is no source pack or attachment for this request; use only the facts here and defensible occupational knowledge, and do not invent incident counts, rates, system capabilities, statutory thresholds, approvals, or vendor commitments. Prepare one justified operating recommendation that compares the two periods, explains which apparent changes may reflect real performance and which may reflect reporting effects, and sets out a defensible transition position and intended end state, including the residual guard coverage that can be supported by the available facts. The armed-intrusion-during-outage situation must have a distinct treatment rather than being absorbed into routine guidance. Date the memorandum as of 30 June 2025, distinguish operational practice from legal advice, and make clear the boundaries of every important operating proposition, including what it does not establish. Address ownership, priorities, timing, dependencies, and the conditions under which the operating position would need reconsideration, but do not claim to authorize deployment, amend a contract, or make a jurisdiction-specific legal determination. Keep the document to six pages or fewer, make it usable by Legal and contracting without asking you to decode it, and finish on the approval decision rather than a list of future steps. Write the completed memorandum to the top level of the working directory in exactly this filename: kestrel_terminals_operating_decision_memo.docx.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `kestrel_terminals_operating_decision_memo.docx` — A briefing-ready doctrinal operating decision memorandum for Legal and contracting that compares the two reporting periods, evaluates the remote-monitoring transition, states one operating recommendation, and treats the outage-and-armed-intrusion exception separately. (Write the file into the top level of the solver's starting working directory.; Use exactly the specified filename.; Do not exceed six rendered pages.; Do not fabricate incident counts, rates, capabilities, legal thresholds, approvals, or contractual commitments.; The memorandum must be self-contained because no reference files are supplied.; End with the approval decision rather than a list of next steps.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/kestrel_terminals_operating_decision_memo.docx","bytes":50462,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/acf64c4f040b9a0558246f4ae27acd9a16a4cd122ed01fda2306398f88c4312f.docx","sha256":"acf64c4f040b9a0558246f4ae27acd9a16a4cd122ed01fda2306398f88c4312f","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"DECISION MEMORANDUM  |  AS OF 30 JUNE 2025\nOperating Decision Memorandum\nKestrel Terminals monitoring transition\nTO\nCecilia Gauthier, Legal  |  Katarzyna Voskresensky, Contracting\nFROM\nNadia Marchetti, First-line Supervisor, Kestrel Terminals\nDATE\n30 June 2025\nSUBJECT\nApproval of a bounded operating position for the fixed-guard to Bramblewood Advisory remote-monitoring transition\nDECISION STATUS\nRecommendation for approval this week; not an authorization to deploy or amend a contract\n\nDECISION REQUESTED\nApprove a staged hybrid transition with a residual physical guard floor.\nUse Bramblewood Advisory remote monitoring as the primary routine detection and assessment channel only when its availability, alert verification, independent communications, and human escalation are demonstrated. Keep the fixed guard coverage already retained during the transition; preserve physical coverage at the highest-consequence access or operating areas whenever remote coverage or escalation is unavailable. Do not remove further posts because logged incidents appear lower. This is a recommendation for Legal and contracting approval, not Nadia Marchetti's authorization to deploy, alter coverage, amend a contract, or make a legal determination.\n\nExecutive judgment\nKestrel Terminals is midway through replacing fixed guard posts with Bramblewood Advisory's remote-monitoring model. The request describes an apparent material change between January-March and April-June, but no incident records or source pack were supplied. I therefore do not state a count, rate, direction, or causal result for either period. The comparison can inform a control decision only after separating underlying security events from changes in what was observable, classified, escalated, and recorded.\nThe rare armed intrusion that occurred while the monitoring system was unavailable is the decisive resilience fact. It does not prove that remote monitoring is generally unsafe; it does establish that loss of the monitoring channel can coincide with a severe event and must change the operating posture. A low or quiet log cannot close that exposure, especially when the reporting regime changed and the absence of an alert is not proof that no event occurred.\nWHY THIS CALL IS NARROW\nThe available facts support a control posture and approval conditions, not a staffing count, system performance claim, cost result, statutory threshold, or vendor commitment.\nWHAT THE APPROVAL WOULD MEAN\nLegal and contracting may approve the operating position for translation into an executable plan by Ivo Cordova and Kaia Yamamoto; implementation authority remains with the designated Kestrel approval process.\n\nDECISION LENS\nThe recommended tradeoff accepts slower transition efficiency and retained guard burden in exchange for avoiding an unverified single point of failure. The end state remains conditional: remote monitoring may become the routine primary channel only after the dependencies in this memo are demonstrated and owned.\n\n\n01  |  EVIDENCE DISCIPLINE\nWhat the two periods can and cannot show\nThe common comparison basis is the same for both periods: (a) underlying security events and exposure, (b) opportunity and coverage available to observe them, (c) detection and alert verification, (d) classification and duplicate handling, (e) escalation and site response, and (f) completeness of the record. Comparing an incident outcome in one period with an unrelated monitoring measure in the other would not support a decision.\nALIGNED LENS\nJANUARY-MARCH\nAPRIL-JUNE\nPeriod context\nJanuary-March is the earlier comparison period. The exact mix of fixed posts and remote coverage by area, shift, and day is not supplied.\nApril-June is the later comparison period while the move toward Bramblewood remote monitoring was still underway. The exact coverage mix and transition milestones are not supplied.\nApparent change\nThe request says the records appear materially different across the two periods; no direction, count, rate, or category breakdown is provided here.\nA later-period difference could reflect a real change in events, a change in observation, or both. It cannot be assigned to the remote model from the brief alone.\nDetection and logging\nFixed presence may expose local observations and immediate interactions that are not captured the same way by remote systems.\nRemote monitoring may surface signals beyond a post, but availability, alert verification, escalation, and record completeness can change what gets noticed and logged.\nClassification\nThe record may use an earlier threshold for incident, alarm, nuisance, duplicate, or unresolved event; the definitions are unknown.\nA changed threshold, category, duplicate rule, or handoff practice can create an apparent trend without a matching change in underlying risk.\nExposure and response\nOperating hours, monitored areas, access activity, weather or seasonal opportunity, and response readiness are not supplied.\nRaw totals are not comparable unless opportunity to observe and the ability to respond were materially understood. No denominator is available.\nDecision value\nThe periods provide a reason to examine the transition and its controls, not a numerical proof of improved safety.\nThe armed intrusion during an outage makes availability and degraded-mode readiness more decision-relevant than a quiet log alone.\n\nInterpretation rules\n• Observed fact: the organization is midway through a transition, the request describes an apparent material period change, and a rare armed intrusion occurred during a monitoring outage. The incident records themselves are not supplied.\n• Professional inference: a change in logged events may be real performance, improved or reduced detection, changed exposure, changed classification, duplicate handling, or incomplete escalation. A busier record can likewise reflect better detection rather than deterioration.\n• Assumption for the recommendation: no further guard reduction is treated as justified unless equivalent observation, communications, escalation, and site-response conditions are demonstrated. This is a management control, not a statutory or legal threshold.\n• Evidence gap: coverage maps, operating hours, outage duration and scope, alert definitions, duplicate rules, response records, unresolved alerts, and the vendor's actual service scope are needed before anyone can claim a trend or set a numerical staffing plan.\nRULE-OF-THUMB BOUNDARY\nFewer logged incidents do not, by themselves, establish lower underlying risk.\nThe common shortcut is to treat a quieter record as proof of improved safety or to remove physical presence once logs decline. It stops being reliable here when reporting conditions changed, exposure or coverage changed, the monitoring channel was degraded or unavailable, or classification and escalation were not held constant. An alert is an indication requiring assessment, not proof of an intrusion; no alert or logged event is not proof that no event occurred.\n\nDecision-relevant finding: the material change that can be acted on now is not an unverified incident trend. It is the control dependency exposed by the transition: routine remote observation must have a tested degraded mode, and the severe outage-and-armed-intrusion case must sit outside routine verification.\n\n\n02  |  ALTERNATIVES AND RECOMMENDATION\nOptions against the approval dimensions\nThe alternatives are compared on safety and resilience, operational coverage, cost or resource burden, and compliance or reputational exposure. None is a guarantee. The comparison is qualitative because the brief supplies no cost data, staffing plan, technical specification, or legal standard.\nOPTION\nSAFETY / RESILIENCE\nOPERATIONAL COVERAGE\nCOST / RESOURCES\nCOMPLIANCE / REPUTATION\nFixed guard posts\nProvides visible presence, local observation, access interaction, and a direct physical fallback. Guards do not guarantee prevention, detection, or a safe outcome.\nCoverage is bounded by post location, visibility, shift presence, and human attention; it may leave other areas or times less observed.\nCarries recurring labor, supervision, scheduling, and potential contract burden; no amount or cost is established.\nA visible control may support confidence and traceability, while duty scope and labor/contract implications require review.\nBramblewood remote monitoring\nMay extend observation and central assessment beyond a fixed post when available and followed by people. It is not equivalent to physical presence and does not guarantee prevention or response.\nDepends on power, communications, sensor/camera availability, alert verification, escalation, and site follow-through. No capability, uptime, response time, staffing, or integration is established.\nCould reduce fixed-post resource burden in a demonstrated end state; no saving, fee, or vendor commitment is supplied.\nConsistent records may help accountability; outages, missed handoffs, or unclear scope can create compliance and reputation exposure.\nStaged hybrid / residual guard\nRetains a physical fallback while remote observation proves dependable and prevents a single channel failure from becoming the whole control.\nUses remote monitoring for routine coverage and physical presence for high-consequence points and degraded mode; exact locations and hours require a site assessment.\nDefers the full guard reduction and keeps resource burden during transition, but avoids unsupported optimization based on a changed log.\nMakes conditions, handoffs, records, and exception treatment visible for Legal and contracting review; it is not a legal conclusion.\n\nSELECTED OPERATING POSITION\nApprove a staged hybrid transition; do not authorize a further guard reduction on record quietness alone.\nRemote monitoring is the preferred routine detection and assessment channel where its dependencies are demonstrated. The fixed guard coverage already retained is the transition floor. It remains available for local observation, deterrence, access or alarm verification where safe, response support, and fallback when the remote channel or human handoff is unavailable. This preserves transition efficiency as an objective while recognizing that the outage event makes residual risk material.\n\nResidual guard coverage: a defensible floor\nThe available facts support retaining the fixed coverage already in place at the approval date, with no further reduction during the transition. They do not support inventing a post count, location, or shift schedule. The executable plan should identify the highest-consequence access or operating area(s) and keep the currently available physical guard coverage assigned to those points whenever remote monitoring, communications, or required human escalation is unavailable. This is a floor to hold while evidence is gathered, not a promise of a particular staffing level.\nThe guard function is bounded: visible deterrence; local observation; safe access or alarm verification under site procedure; communication and response support; and a physical fallback during degraded coverage. Guards cannot guarantee prevention, detect every event, make a remote view equivalent to presence, or assure a safe outcome. During an armed intrusion, they do not revert to routine alarm verification; the separate exception doctrine on page 5 controls.\nThis position is proportionate to the evidence. It preserves the protective value of presence while avoiding a claim that either guards or cameras are complete controls. It also keeps the decision reversible: if availability, response readiness, or exposure changes, the posture returns to the residual-guard floor without treating that trigger as a legal threshold.\n\n\n03  |  TRANSITION OPERATING POSITION\nWho does what, and when the position can be relied upon\nThe following is operational practice for approval and planning. It is not legal advice. Legal determines applicable law and interpretation; contracting determines what Bramblewood's executed scope and remedies say. No service level, staffing commitment, integration, or approval is assumed because none is supplied in the brief.\nOWNER\nACCOUNTABILITY / HANDOFF\nBOUNDARY: WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH\nBramblewood Advisory\nObserve and assess signals within the agreed scope; distinguish an indication from a confirmed event; record availability, alerts, handoffs, and unresolved items; escalate through the approved primary route and an independent route when the primary monitoring path is unavailable.\nThis does not establish that Bramblewood currently has any named capability, response time, uptime, staffing, or integration. It does not transfer site-safety ownership or authorize a contract change.\nSite personnel / retained guards\nReceive a handoff, protect immediate safety, perform only safe and authorized local verification or access control, provide response support, and report facts to Kestrel management. Use the degraded or emergency posture when the remote channel cannot be relied upon.\nThis does not require confrontation, guarantee a safe outcome, or make a guard responsible for restoring the monitoring system. Armed-intrusion actions follow the separate emergency doctrine and established site procedures.\nKestrel Terminals management\nOwn the site risk posture, coverage decision, operating procedures, guard readiness, authoritative incident and outage record, escalation to leadership, and decision to return from degraded mode. Ivo Cordova and Kaia Yamamoto can translate this position into the executable operating plan.\nThis does not authorize Nadia Marchetti to deploy, reduce coverage, amend a contract, or make a jurisdiction-specific legal determination.\nLegal and contracting\nReview the proposed operating position, applicable legal questions, scope, records, notice and performance provisions, and any required approval or change instrument before reliance on the end state.\nThis review is not the immediate operational response to an armed intrusion or outage. Legal and contracting do not replace site safety leadership, emergency response, or operational escalation.\n\nConditions for dependable routine remote monitoring\nDEPENDENCY\nOPERATING CONDITION\nBOUNDARY\nAvailability and coverage\nThe monitored areas, operating periods, power/comms dependencies, outage detection, and recovery status are known and recorded.\nA system being installed or later restored does not by itself prove that the site is safe or that coverage is complete.\nAssessment and escalation\nSignals have agreed operational definitions, are assessed rather than treated as proof, and have a human handoff path for verified or unresolved concerns.\nNo numeric response promise or legal threshold is created by this control.\nIndependent communications\nA route independent of the monitoring system can reach site leadership, retained guards/site personnel, and public emergency response as appropriate to the site plan.\nThe memo does not claim that a particular radio, phone, or emergency service is available or sufficient.\nHuman follow-through\nNamed Kestrel personnel or guards can receive, safely verify where appropriate, escalate, and close or leave open an event; unresolved alerts remain visible.\nRemote observation is not physical presence and does not guarantee prevention or a safe outcome.\nRecords and assurance\nKestrel and Bramblewood use aligned event, duplicate, outage, and closure definitions and preserve records needed to compare periods and review exceptions.\nA complete-looking log is not proof that no unobserved event occurred.\n\nTransition state versus intended end state\nTRANSITION STATE | APPLIES NOW\nKeep the retained fixed guard coverage; use remote monitoring as a conditional routine channel; enter degraded mode when availability, communications, or human escalation is unavailable; review every proposed coverage change against the conditions above.\nINTENDED END STATE | CONDITIONAL\nRemote monitoring is the routine primary detection and assessment channel, with only risk-justified residual physical coverage and a tested severe-exception posture. It is not complete or reliable merely because the migration is underway or the log is quieter.\n\n\n\n04  |  SEPARATE SEVERE EXCEPTION\nArmed intrusion during a monitoring-system outage\nEXCEPTION DOCTRINE\nTreat this as a rare, high-consequence emergency and a degraded operating state, not as another routine alarm.\nThe fact pattern is an armed intrusion occurring while the monitoring system was unavailable. Its rarity does not make it an ordinary data point: the potential consequence is immediate harm to life and a simultaneous loss of normal detection and escalation. Protection of life and immediate site safety take priority over routine monitoring, reporting, ticket closure, or contract administration.\n\nRequired posture\n01  Protect life first. Site leadership, guards, and personnel follow the established emergency and protective procedures for the site and the circumstances. The physical response posture is protective and emergency-oriented, not a routine approach to verify an alarm. Tactical choices belong to trained and authorized responders; this memo supplies no jurisdiction-specific or tactical instruction.\n02  Use an independent route. When the monitoring system is unavailable, use an alternate communications path that does not depend on that failed channel to reach site leadership, retained guards or site personnel, and public emergency response as appropriate. Do not wait for Bramblewood to restore monitoring before making an urgent safety notification. The existence or adequacy of a particular route is not established here and must be confirmed in the operating plan.\n03  Separate operational escalation from review. Kestrel site leadership owns immediate protective coordination and escalation. Bramblewood reports known facts and the outage status through any available independent route and preserves its records. Legal and contracting are notified for later interpretation, preservation, scope, and performance review; they do not replace operational command or emergency response.\n04  Preserve facts after safety is addressed. Preserve the outage timeline and status, monitoring and access records, communications, notifications, contemporaneous accounts, and relevant scene or evidence information under applicable site procedures. This supports later review but does not determine liability, evidentiary admissibility, or a contractual breach.\n05  Return only after readiness is re-established. Return to ordinary monitoring only after the immediate hazard is addressed by authorized responders or site leadership, the monitoring and independent communications paths are separately checked, the required physical or human response coverage is available, and the facts are preserved and reviewed. Restoration or clearance of the monitoring outage alone is not proof that the site is safe and does not close the exception.\nWHAT THE EXCEPTION ESTABLISHES\nAn outage is a material operating dependency. The transition must have an independent communications path, a physical or human fallback, and an explicit return-to-readiness decision.\nWHAT IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH\nIt does not prove that every remote-monitoring period is unsafe, that a guard would have prevented the event, or that the event supplies a legal or contractual conclusion.\n\nOrdinary degraded mode outside the severe exception\nFor a monitoring, communications, or human-response outage without a known armed intrusion, move to the residual-guard floor, use the independent route, keep unresolved alerts open, and obtain management confirmation before resuming routine reliance. This is a lower posture than the severe exception, but it is still a meaningful change from normal remote monitoring. It is an operational control and not a statutory threshold.\n\n\n05  |  APPROVAL DECISION\nThe operating call for this approval week\nThe evidence is insufficient to declare that January-March was safer or less safe than April-June, and historical incident records alone cannot establish that Bramblewood's model is safe or effective. The reporting regime changed during the transition, and the outage event shows why availability and response readiness matter independently of incident counts. The recommendation therefore follows from resilience and evidence discipline, not from a claimed trend.\nApproval conditions to carry into the executable plan\nDIMENSION\nAPPROVAL POSITION\nBOUNDARY\nPriority\nProtect life and maintain a credible physical or human fallback whenever the remote channel cannot be relied upon.\nThis prioritization does not authorize a tactical response, promise a safe outcome, or displace applicable emergency procedures.\nOwnership\nKestrel owns site risk, coverage posture, records, and return-to-readiness; Bramblewood owns monitoring actions and records only within the agreed scope; Legal and contracting review interpretation and terms.\nNo party is assigned an unstated contractual duty by this memo.\nTiming\nAdopt the transition position for approval this week; hold the retained guard floor through transition; consider an end state only after the dependencies are demonstrated and documented.\n“Demonstrated” is a management decision criterion, not a statutory, regulatory, or legal threshold.\nReconsideration\nRe-enter the residual-guard or severe-exception posture after unavailability, failed communications or handoff, material exposure change, unresolved event concern, or an armed intrusion during outage.\nA trigger requires assessment; it does not by itself prove causation, liability, or a breach.\n\nDECISION STATEMENT\nAPPROVE THE STAGED HYBRID OPERATING POSITION.\nLegal and contracting should approve, for implementation planning, the use of Bramblewood Advisory remote monitoring as the conditional routine primary channel, retention of existing residual fixed guard coverage through the transition, mandatory degraded-mode handling when monitoring, communications, or human escalation is unavailable, and the separate life-safety doctrine for an armed intrusion during an outage. Nadia Marchetti presents this bounded recommendation; she does not independently authorize deployment, reduce coverage, amend Bramblewood's contract, or decide a jurisdiction-specific legal question. The approval decision is to proceed on this bounded operating basis.\n\nNadia Marchetti\nFirst-line Supervisor, Kestrel Terminals","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is named exactly kestrel_terminals_operating_decision_memo.docx.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Submitted filename and filesystem metadata.","grader_hint":"Check the top-level submitted file name character-for-character, including extension."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX opens successfully and renders as a readable memorandum.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opened DOCX and rendered pages.","grader_hint":"Open and render the document with an offline office converter; award only if text and essential structure are intact."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered memorandum is no longer than six pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Count rendered pages, not the nominal DOCX section count."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document clearly identifies itself as an operating or decision memorandum concerning Kestrel Terminals' monitoring transition.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title and opening text.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent professional wording; the subject must be unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum is explicitly addressed to the Legal and contracting audience rather than to a generic security reader.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"To/from or audience language and document framing.","grader_hint":"Look for Cecilia Gauthier and Katarzyna Voskresensky or an equally clear Legal-and-contracting audience designation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum states an as-of date of 30 June 2025 or an unmistakably equivalent date.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Date line or scope statement.","grader_hint":"Accept ISO or long-form rendering of the same date."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states the approval decision that Legal and contracting are being asked to consider.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive summary and conclusion.","grader_hint":"The reader should be able to state what operating position is being presented for approval without inference."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum makes one clear operating recommendation rather than presenting several unresolved options as co-equal conclusions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation language and conclusion.","grader_hint":"A conditional recommendation is acceptable only if it still identifies one preferred operating position."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Both January–March and April–June are explicitly identified as the comparison periods.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison section or table.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent quarter labels if the months are clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison uses a common conceptual basis for both periods and does not compare an incident outcome in one period with an unrelated operational measure in the other.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison narrative and any table.","grader_hint":"Check that both periods are discussed using aligned event, exposure, detection, or response concepts."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum connects the period comparison to the chronology of the move from fixed guard posts toward Bramblewood Advisory remote monitoring.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition context and comparison narrative.","grader_hint":"The transition must be treated as an interpretive factor, not merely as background branding."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not present invented incident counts, rates, percentages, or monetary results as observed facts.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All quantitative claims in the rendered document.","grader_hint":"Search tables, prose, and callouts for unsupported numbers; qualitative comparisons and explicitly labeled illustrative formulas are acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not promise or state as established any Bramblewood Advisory system capability, uptime, response time, staffing level, or integration that the brief does not establish.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Capability and service-level claims.","grader_hint":"Award when capabilities are framed as assumptions, requirements, or items to verify rather than as known facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document plainly acknowledges that no source pack was supplied and explains how that limits the certainty of the period comparison.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Basis, limitations, or assumptions statement.","grader_hint":"A generic disclaimer is insufficient; it must connect the absence of source records to the strength of conclusions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The comparison explains that the monitoring transition can change the reporting regime, including what is detected, classified, escalated, or recorded.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for at least two concrete reporting-mechanism effects, not just the phrase reporting bias."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes changes in underlying security events from changes in the probability that events are noticed or logged.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interpretation of period differences.","grader_hint":"The distinction must affect the conclusion or confidence level, not appear as an isolated definition."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document recognizes that changes in incident categories, thresholds, duplicate handling, or classification practice can create apparent trends.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions and data-quality discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept any defensible treatment of classification or record-construction effects."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The comparison addresses exposure or denominator changes, such as operating hours, monitored areas, coverage availability, or opportunity to observe events.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and limitations.","grader_hint":"The memo need not calculate a rate, but it must explain why raw event totals may not be comparable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies which changes between the periods are material to the operating decision and explains why they matter.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Findings and recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"A mere chronology or list of incident types does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document separates observed facts, professional inferences, assumptions, and unresolved evidence gaps.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Throughout the memorandum, especially findings and limitations.","grader_hint":"Use of labels or disciplined wording is acceptable; the categories must be distinguishable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The assessment considers leading indicators of monitoring and security performance in addition to incident counts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Measures or decision criteria.","grader_hint":"Examples include availability, alarm verification, detection coverage, response readiness, or unresolved alerts; do not require a particular metric list."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum explains why historical incident records alone are insufficient to establish that the remote-monitoring model is safe or effective.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence limitations and recommendation basis.","grader_hint":"This should be tied to both the reporting change and the outage event."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document identifies at least one plausible alternative explanation for an apparent improvement or deterioration and states how that uncertainty affects the decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interpretive analysis.","grader_hint":"Accept operational, reporting, exposure, or seasonal explanations if they are connected to decision confidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The fixed-guard-post alternative is described fairly, including its protective value and at least one operational or financial limitation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternatives comparison.","grader_hint":"The incumbent option must not be caricatured as either automatically safe or automatically unacceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The Bramblewood Advisory remote-monitoring alternative is described fairly, including its potential value and its dependence on reliable technology, escalation, and human follow-through.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternatives comparison.","grader_hint":"Look for a balanced capability-and-dependency treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"r26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A residual-guard or hybrid operating position is considered as a distinct alternative rather than being omitted from the comparison.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternatives section and recommendation.","grader_hint":"The document need not select hybrid coverage, but must assess it as a genuine option."},{"rubric_item_id":"r27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The alternatives are compared against explicit decision dimensions that include safety or resilience, operational coverage, cost or resource burden, and compliance or reputational exposure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison table or prose.","grader_hint":"Award only when the dimensions are actually applied to the options."},{"rubric_item_id":"r28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation makes the tradeoff between transition efficiency and residual risk visible instead of claiming that one option dominates on every dimension.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"The tradeoff must be specific to this transition and outage exposure."},{"rubric_item_id":"r29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The stated recommendation follows from the period-analysis limitations, alternative comparison, and outage exception rather than contradicting them.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Findings, options, and recommendation read together.","grader_hint":"Trace the reasoning across sections; do not award if the conclusion ignores a material caveat already acknowledged."},{"rubric_item_id":"r30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document gives a defensible rationale for the amount and purpose of residual guard coverage during the transition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition operating position.","grader_hint":"A bare statement to retain or remove guards is insufficient; assess risk, coverage, and the guard function."},{"rubric_item_id":"r31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes what residual guards are expected to do from what they cannot guarantee.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Guard coverage discussion.","grader_hint":"Look for bounded duties such as deterrence, physical presence, verification, or response support paired with limitations."},{"rubric_item_id":"r32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document states the operational conditions on which remote monitoring is dependable, including system availability, alert verification, communications, and human escalation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Remote-monitoring assessment.","grader_hint":"At least three relevant dependencies should be treated as conditions, not assumed facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"r33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The transition-state operating position is distinguished from the intended end-state model.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition and end-state sections.","grader_hint":"The reader should be able to tell what applies while the migration is incomplete versus after conditions are met."},{"rubric_item_id":"r34","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not treat the intended end state as already reliable or complete when the brief says the organization is only midway through transition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state claims.","grader_hint":"Award when the end state is conditional, staged, or otherwise bounded by demonstrated capability."},{"rubric_item_id":"r35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommended operating position assigns substantive ownership to Kestrel Terminals and Bramblewood Advisory rather than leaving accountability with an unnamed team.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles and responsibilities language.","grader_hint":"Ownership may be split by detection, site response, system assurance, records, or approval, but must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum describes the handoff and accountability boundary between remote monitoring, site personnel or guards, and Kestrel Terminals management.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operating model and responsibility section.","grader_hint":"Look for who receives, verifies, escalates, and closes an event or outage."},{"rubric_item_id":"r37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation identifies a concrete priority and timing for the approval-week decision and for the transition position to take effect, without pretending that an implementation has already been authorized.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and implementation boundary.","grader_hint":"A time horizon such as immediate, transition period, or end state is sufficient if tied to an owner or decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"r38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document identifies dependencies that Legal or contracting must understand before the operating position can be relied upon.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dependencies, conditions, or approval considerations.","grader_hint":"Examples include service scope, outage reporting, response obligations, records, or coverage commitments."},{"rubric_item_id":"r39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum states what happens to the operating posture when monitoring coverage, communications, or required human response is unavailable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Contingency or degraded-mode discussion.","grader_hint":"The response must be an operationally meaningful change in posture, not merely an instruction to monitor the issue."},{"rubric_item_id":"r40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document recognizes the widely used rule of thumb that fewer logged incidents indicate improved safety or that remote monitoring can replace physical presence once logs decline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rule-of-thumb discussion.","grader_hint":"Equivalent wording is acceptable if it captures the common shortcut being tested."},{"rubric_item_id":"r41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum states concrete conditions under which that rule of thumb stops being reliable in this setting.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Boundary or applicability discussion.","grader_hint":"Conditions should include changed reporting or detection and degraded or unavailable monitoring; do not award for a generic caveat alone."},{"rubric_item_id":"r42","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not use a decline in logged incidents by itself as proof that underlying security risk has declined.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims about period performance.","grader_hint":"Search for unsupported causal language linking quieter records directly to safety."},{"rubric_item_id":"r43","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The armed-intrusion-during-outage exception is visibly separated from the general operating guidance.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered headings, callout, or clearly separated prose.","grader_hint":"A reader should be able to find the exception without reading the whole routine model."},{"rubric_item_id":"r44","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The separate exception treatment explicitly addresses an armed intrusion occurring while the monitoring system was unavailable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception section text.","grader_hint":"Both the armed intrusion and the system outage must be present in the same exception logic."},{"rubric_item_id":"r45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum explains why the exception is rare but severe enough to require a different operating posture.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception rationale.","grader_hint":"Look for consequence and severity, not just the adjective rare."},{"rubric_item_id":"r46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception doctrine places protection of life and immediate safety ahead of routine monitoring, reporting, or contract-administration concerns.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception response principles.","grader_hint":"Do not require tactical detail; require an unmistakable safety priority."},{"rubric_item_id":"r47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception treatment identifies an independent or alternate communications path when the monitoring system is unavailable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Outage-and-intrusion response doctrine.","grader_hint":"A generic reference to escalation is insufficient unless it can function independently of the failed system."},{"rubric_item_id":"r48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes the physical response and site-safety posture for the armed-intrusion exception from routine alarm verification.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception section.","grader_hint":"Award for a clear distinction in response posture without requiring jurisdiction-specific tactical instructions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r49","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document identifies appropriate notification and escalation boundaries for the severe exception, including the distinction between operational escalation and Legal or contracting review.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception governance language.","grader_hint":"The memo should not imply that Legal or contracting replaces immediate safety response."},{"rubric_item_id":"r50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception treatment addresses preservation of incident facts, records, or evidence after the immediate safety concern.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Post-event doctrine.","grader_hint":"Accept operational record preservation, scene integrity, or equivalent evidence-conscious language."},{"rubric_item_id":"r51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum states what must be established before returning from the degraded or exception posture to ordinary monitoring operations.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recovery or return-to-service language.","grader_hint":"Look for verification of system, communications, staffing, or risk conditions rather than an automatic return."},{"rubric_item_id":"r52","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not bury the severe outage-and-intrusion response inside routine guidance or treat it as merely another alarm category.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document structure and exception logic.","grader_hint":"This item is about substantive separation, not merely the presence of an exception heading."},{"rubric_item_id":"r53","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum states or clearly implies that a monitoring alert is an indication requiring assessment, not proof that an intrusion occurred.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions and operating rules.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent distinction between signal, verification, and confirmed event."},{"rubric_item_id":"r54","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not claim that remote camera or sensor observation is equivalent to physical security presence or guaranteed prevention.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Remote-monitoring claims.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit capability boundary, not simply absence of boastful language."},{"rubric_item_id":"r55","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not treat the absence of an alert or logged incident as proof that no intrusion or safety event occurred.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims about negative evidence.","grader_hint":"The distinction may appear in the data limitations or exception section."},{"rubric_item_id":"r56","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not claim that the presence of guards guarantees prevention, detection, or a safe outcome.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Guard coverage claims.","grader_hint":"Accept a bounded statement of guard value paired with its limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"r57","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum does not treat restoration or clearance of the monitoring outage as proof that the site is immediately safe or that the exception is closed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Outage recovery claims.","grader_hint":"Look for separate verification of operational and safety conditions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r58","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any operating trigger, service expectation, or review point is not presented as a statutory, regulatory, or legal threshold.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Threshold and trigger language.","grader_hint":"Award when operational criteria are labeled as management controls or recommendations rather than law."},{"rubric_item_id":"r59","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum avoids unsupported jurisdiction-specific legal conclusions and clearly directs legal interpretation to the appropriate Legal audience where needed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal caveats and claims.","grader_hint":"Do not require a disclaimer on every page; assess whether the overall document stays within operational authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"r60","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Each important operating rule is paired with a clear statement of what that rule does not establish or authorize.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operating rules and their boundaries throughout the memo.","grader_hint":"Assess the pairing as a pattern across the document; isolated caveats do not satisfy the doctrinal requirement."},{"rubric_item_id":"r61","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The author presents the recommendation for approval and does not claim that Nadia Marchetti has independently authorized deployment, changed coverage, amended a contract, or made a legal determination.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Authority statements and conclusion.","grader_hint":"The document may recommend approval and describe an executable operating position, but must preserve the approval boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"r62","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum ends with a definite approval decision or decision statement rather than a generic invitation to discuss or an unprioritized next-steps list.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final section or final paragraph.","grader_hint":"The final substantive sentence should resolve the operating call and its bounded exception posture."},{"rubric_item_id":"r63","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A Legal or contracting reader can understand the recommendation, its conditions, owners, and limits without asking the author to decode unexplained operational shorthand.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole rendered memorandum.","grader_hint":"Judge usability, not merely grammatical correctness."},{"rubric_item_id":"r64","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The terminology for fixed guards, remote monitoring, outage, incident, transition, end state, and the severe exception remains consistent across the comparison, recommendation, and conclusion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-section terminology audit.","grader_hint":"Do not penalize ordinary synonyms unless they create ambiguity or change the apparent operating meaning."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a high-consequence security-operations decision memorandum for a single terminal operator moving from fixed guard coverage toward remote monitoring. The author has supervisory operational expertise but presents a recommendation for Legal and contracting approval rather than independently authorizing implementation.","difficulty_sources":["The two reporting periods cannot be interpreted naively because the monitoring transition changes observability and classification.","A familiar low-incident rule of thumb must be bounded rather than accepted as proof of improved safety.","The rare armed intrusion during a monitoring outage requires a separate severe-exception doctrine.","The operating recommendation must distinguish transition coverage from an end-state promise while remaining within a supervisor's approval authority.","Dependent outputs must agree: period interpretation, alternative comparison, residual guard position, exception doctrine, ownership, and final approval call."],"authoring_plan":{"mode":"doctrinal","reference_strategy":"No reference files are to be created. The solver must work from the workplace facts in the request and occupational knowledge of first-line security supervision. The gold is a self-contained DOCX memorandum, not a data-analysis workbook and not a fabricated incident report.","facts_to_preserve":["Kestrel Terminals is transitioning from fixed guard posts toward Bramblewood Advisory remote monitoring.","The comparison concerns January–March and April–June.","The transition changes the event-detection, classification, escalation, and logging environment.","A rare armed intrusion occurred during a monitoring-system outage.","The outage-and-intrusion event is severe enough to require a distinct doctrine.","Approval is needed during the current week.","Nadia Marchetti presents the operating recommendation; Cecilia Gauthier represents Legal; Katarzyna Voskresensky represents contracting; Ivo Cordova and Kaia Yamamoto are operational stakeholders awaiting an executable position.","There are no supplied incident records, so the gold must not invent numerical observations."],"doctrinal_expectations":{"period_comparison":["Compare the periods on aligned concepts rather than merely listing events.","Explain that a monitoring-model change can alter observability, classification, duplicate handling, escalation, and reporting completeness.","Separate underlying event frequency from detection and recording frequency.","Discuss exposure or denominator changes and the limits of raw incident totals.","Use leading indicators such as availability, coverage, alert verification, response readiness, and unresolved events without inventing values.","Distinguish observed facts, inference, assumptions, and evidence gaps.","State at least one plausible alternative explanation for an apparent trend."],"alternatives_and_recommendation":["Fairly compare fixed guard posts, remote monitoring, and a hybrid or residual-guard position.","Apply safety or resilience, operational coverage, cost or resource burden, and compliance or reputational exposure as decision dimensions.","Make one preferred operating recommendation.","Explain why the recommendation follows despite uncertainty in the period records.","Give residual guards a bounded purpose and do not imply that guards guarantee prevention.","Distinguish the transition operating position from the intended end state.","Make the end state conditional on demonstrated monitoring, communications, response, and accountability capability.","Assign substantive ownership and handoff responsibilities between Kestrel Terminals and Bramblewood Advisory.","Identify timing, priorities, dependencies, and degraded-coverage conditions."],"rule_of_thumb_boundary":["The gold should identify the common shortcut that fewer logged incidents or quieter records demonstrate lower risk or justify removing physical presence.","It should state when the shortcut ceases to be reliable, especially when reporting conditions changed, exposure changed, or monitoring is degraded.","It must not use a decline in logged incidents alone as proof of improved safety."],"severe_exception":["Use a visibly distinct treatment for the armed intrusion during monitoring outage.","Treat it as rare but high-consequence and not interchangeable with a routine alert.","Prioritize life safety and immediate site safety.","Provide an independent or alternate communications route when the monitoring system is unavailable.","Distinguish physical response posture from routine remote verification without adding tactical violence instructions or jurisdiction-specific procedures.","Separate operational escalation from Legal and contracting review.","Address preservation of facts, records, and evidence after immediate safety is addressed.","State conditions for return from degraded or exception posture to ordinary operations.","Do not let outage recovery alone close the exception or prove the site safe."],"doctrinal_nonclaims":["An alert is an indication requiring assessment, not proof of an intrusion.","A camera or sensor view is not equivalent to physical security presence.","No alert or logged event is not proof that no event occurred.","Guard presence is not a guarantee of prevention or safe outcome.","An outage being cleared is not by itself proof of safety or restored readiness.","Operational triggers are not statutory or legal thresholds.","The document must not assert unsupported legal rules, vendor capabilities, service levels, approvals, or contractual commitments."]},"document_constraints":["The gold must render at six pages or fewer.","The document should be briefing-ready and self-contained for Legal and contracting.","The final substantive passage must resolve the approval decision, not become a list of future steps.","The memo must preserve the as-of date of 30 June 2025."],"gold_derivation":"Have a domain-expert author create a qualitative decision memo satisfying the above doctrine without numerical answer keys. Review the rendered artifact against every rubric item, especially the distinction between reporting effects and real performance, the boundary of the rule of thumb, the separate outage-and-armed-intrusion doctrine, and the paired must-not claims. Verify that no unsupported numbers or legal assertions appear and that the recommendation, ownership, timing, transition position, end state, and conclusion agree."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"operating_decision_memo","filename":"kestrel_terminals_operating_decision_memo.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A briefing-ready doctrinal operating decision memorandum for Legal and contracting that compares the two reporting periods, evaluates the remote-monitoring transition, states one operating recommendation, and treats the outage-and-armed-intrusion exception separately.","required":true,"constraints":["Write the file into the top level of the solver's starting working directory.","Use exactly the specified filename.","Do not exceed six rendered pages.","Do not fabricate incident counts, rates, capabilities, legal thresholds, approvals, or contractual commitments.","The memorandum must be self-contained because no reference files are supplied.","End with the approval decision rather than a list of next steps."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"A domain-expert reviewer should render the DOCX and inspect it against every rubric item, with particular attention to unsupported factual or legal claims, doctrinal boundaries, and whether the final recommendation is singular and internally consistent.","minimum_reviewers":1,"known_limitations":["Because no source records are supplied, the gold and rubric assess qualitative comparison discipline and defensible operating doctrine rather than numerical period results.","No jurisdiction-specific legal conclusion is intended; legal-boundary items assess restraint and referral, not a particular statute or threshold."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:20:27+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:20:27+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:20:27+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:20:27+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/9b546d8180a26105","declared_deliverables":["kestrel_terminals_operating_decision_memo.docx"]};
